## Method and reasoning
Reduce a modulo m. Process exponent bits from low to high. At bit i, the current base represents a raised to 2ⁱ. Multiply it into the residue when the bit is set, then square it. Use unsigned 128-bit products before reduction so multiplying two full-width 64-bit residues remains exact.

## Walk through the sample
In the final query the base equals m−1. Its square is congruent to 1, even though the unreduced product exceeds 64 bits. The modulus-1 query also checks that the exponent-zero identity is reduced.

## Complexity
O(log(b+1)+1) time and O(1) extra space per query.

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

using U64 = uint64_t;
using U128 = __uint128_t;
int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int q;
    cin >> q;
    while (q--) {
        U64 a, b, m;
        cin >> a >> b >> m;
        a %= m;
        U64 answer = 1 % m;
        for (; b; b >>= 1) {
            if (b & 1)
                answer = U128(answer) * a % m;
            a = U128(a) * a % m;
        }
        cout << answer << '\n';
    }
}
